{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794409033,"nonce":"CHqQgWoYyhKc5g6T2nsGQ55Twj0=","message":"Atomics, asked for by the user on 2026-11-11 with the condition that hxn use what Haxe already has if that works with Rust and C++. It does, and that is what was built. The approach is in docs/design.md under \"Atomics\".\n\nThe experiment that decided it (scratch copy, not in the repository): Haxe's own atomic operations on a Rust `AtomicI32`, four Haxe threads and one Rust thread adding at once, then a swap and a store from Haxe; and a `haxe.atomic.AtomicInt` lent to Rust. The total was exact and each side saw the other's writes on Ash in hybrid, jit and interp modes, on stock HashLink 1.15.0 and on hxcpp 4.3.2. On HashLink the operations are `hl.Atomics` on an `hl.Ref\u003cInt\u003e`; on hxcpp `haxe.atomic.AtomicInt` is itself an abstract over a pointer.\n\nWhat was built:\n\n- hxn-abi: `AtomicFlag`, a transparent wrapper over `AtomicI32` that is 0 or 1. A Haxe atomic boolean is an integer cell, and Rust's `AtomicBool` is one byte, so a flag that crosses cannot be that.\n- Haxe: `hxn.AtomicInt` and `hxn.AtomicBool`, with the operations of `haxe.atomic`. One made with `new` owns its memory; one made with `over` works on memory someone else owns and keeps that owner referenced.\n- Generator: `\u0026AtomicI32` and `\u0026AtomicFlag` as parameters (lent for the call), `Arc\u003cAtomicI32\u003e` and `Arc\u003cAtomicFlag\u003e` as results (kept by the library, reached through one `\u003clibrary\u003e_atomic_cell` call that gives the address). A returned cell has no `close()`.\n\nTested through `Generated.hx` on Ash in hybrid, jit and interp modes, stock HashLink and hxcpp: cells the library owns read and written from Haxe; a flag set in Haxe seen by the library; cells made in Haxe lent to a call; a cell outliving the object it came from; and a thread of the library's own watched and stopped through two cells.\n\nNot covered:\n\n- Wasm. `hl.Atomics` on Ash wasm and `_hx_atomic_*` under Emscripten have not been tried; the hand-written fixture, which is what runs there, has no atomics.\n- Only 32-bit integers and flags. No 64-bit cell and no atomic reference.\n- On HashLink the standard `haxe.atomic.AtomicInt` needs `-D hl-ver=1.13.0` or later to compile. `hxn.AtomicInt` does not use it there and needs no define.\n- A Haxe-made cell on hxcpp points into a Haxe array, which is safe while the collector does not move it; hxcpp does not by default.\n- The hand-written bindings have no atomics; only generated ones do.\n\nShared pointers, the other half of the same request, are not built: `#[hxn::interface(shared)]` with `Arc\u003cT\u003e` in signatures, `same()` on the Haxe class, and weak references.","files":null}]}